<!-- §01 Branch Isolation -->
<section id="s-isolation" data-code="isolation">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Branch Isolation Problem</div>
<h2>Tree Search Branches Are Information Silos</h2>
<p>Automated ML engineering agents treat the search for an optimal solution as a tree: each branch represents a chain of iterative improvements, and MCTS decides which branch to expand next. This works reasonably well in the short term — but over a 12-hour search horizon, branches accumulate significant knowledge <em>that they cannot share with each other</em>.</p>
<p>Imagine four branches simultaneously optimizing a Kaggle image classification task. Branch A discovers that adding a custom augmentation pipeline boosts accuracy from 0.82 to 0.847. Branch C and D are stuck at 0.77 and 0.76 respectively — cycling through similar modifications without improving. In standard MCTS, they have no mechanism to observe Branch A's breakthrough. They'll keep stagnating, consuming search budget, while the answer already exists one branch over.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">branch-isolation.canvas — MCTS Tree vs. MCGS Graph</span>
    <div class="canvas-btns">
      <button class="canvas-btn active" id="btn-mcts" onclick="setBranchMode('mcts')">MCTS Tree</button>
      <button class="canvas-btn" id="btn-mcgs" onclick="setBranchMode('mcgs')">MCGS Graph</button>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="padding:.75rem">
    <canvas id="branch-canvas" style="width:100%;height:300px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The fix is simple to describe and surprisingly powerful in practice: make the search space a <strong>directed graph</strong> instead of a tree. Add <em>reference edges</em> (E<sub>ref</sub>) that connect stagnated nodes to top-performing nodes in other branches. Branch C can now query "what are the top 3 nodes across all branches?" and use them as context when generating its next solution attempt — breaking out of its local optimum without modifying the tree structure that governs credit assignment.</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 Progressive MCGS Graph Structure -->
<section id="s-mcgs" data-code="mcgs">
<div class="section-no">02 — Progressive Monte Carlo Graph Search</div>
<h2>Four Ways a Node Can Learn from the Past</h2>
<p>MLEvolve's graph G = (V, E) keeps the familiar MCTS primary-edge tree backbone intact for selection and backpropagation. The innovation is in <em>expansion</em>: when generating a new node, the agent can reference any subset of existing nodes through reference edges E<sub>ref</sub>. These edges do not participate in backpropagation — they're information conduits, not credit paths.</p>
<p>There are four expansion types, triggered in response to stagnation signals:</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">expansion-types.svg — 4 graph expansion modes</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">stagnation level determines which type fires</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 680 320" style="width:100%;height:auto;font-family:var(--ff-mono)" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
      <!-- Background cards -->
      <rect x="10" y="10" width="148" height="290" rx="8" fill="rgba(26,26,46,.8)" stroke="rgba(139,92,246,.25)" stroke-width="1"/>
      <rect x="176" y="10" width="148" height="290" rx="8" fill="rgba(26,26,46,.8)" stroke="rgba(6,182,212,.25)" stroke-width="1"/>
      <rect x="342" y="10" width="148" height="290" rx="8" fill="rgba(26,26,46,.8)" stroke="rgba(245,158,11,.25)" stroke-width="1"/>
      <rect x="508" y="10" width="162" height="290" rx="8" fill="rgba(26,26,46,.8)" stroke="rgba(16,185,129,.25)" stroke-width="1"/>
      <!-- Type 1: Primary -->
      <text x="84" y="32" text-anchor="middle" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="9" font-weight="700" letter-spacing="1">PRIMARY</text>
      <text x="84" y="46" text-anchor="middle" fill="#6b7280" font-size="8">no references</text>
      <!-- Node diagram -->
      <circle cx="84" cy="90" r="10" fill="rgba(139,92,246,.3)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.5"/>
