<p>The elegance of this design is that backpropagation only flows through primary edges — credit assignment remains well-defined on the tree backbone. Reference edges exist purely to enrich the <em>context</em> available when generating the next solution, not to confuse who deserves the reward for an improvement.</p>
</section>

<!-- §03 Progressive Exploration Schedule -->
<section id="s-entropy" data-code="schedule">
<div class="section-no">03 — Entropy-Inspired Progressive Schedule</div>
<h2>From Broad Exploration to Focused Exploitation</h2>
<p>Standard MCTS uses a fixed exploration constant <em>c</em> throughout the search. This is efficient early on — you want to explore many branches — but wasteful late on: once two or three branches have clearly outperformed the rest, spending budget on the weak ones is irrational.</p>
<p>MLEvolve introduces a time-dependent soft switch. At each step, the agent uses UCT-based selection with probability <em>w(t)</em> and Elite-Guided selection (directly sampling from the global top-K nodes, weighted by inverse rank) with probability <em>1 − w(t)</em>. As search progresses, <em>w(t)</em> decreases from 1.0 toward a minimum threshold, gradually concentrating resources on the most promising branches.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Selection strategy at step t</div>
  P(S<sub>t</sub> = UCT) = <span class="math-var">w(t)</span> → 1.0 at start, <span class="math-var">w<sub>min</sub></span> at end<br>
  P(S<sub>t</sub> = Elite) = 1 − <span class="math-var">w(t)</span><br>
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment">// Elite-Guided: sample from global top-K by inverse rank</span><br>
  P(v<sub>i</sub> | elite set) = (1/rank(v<sub>i</sub>)) / Σ (1/rank(v<sub>j</sub>))
</div>

<p>The effect can be measured empirically using Shannon entropy. Within a sliding window at search progress <em>t</em>, compute the empirical distribution π<sub>t</sub> of which branches are selected. The quantity exp(H(π<sub>t</sub>)) is the "effective number of active branches." Drag the slider below to see how it evolves:</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">entropy-dynamics.canvas — effective branch count vs. search progress</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">drag slider to see exploration → exploitation transition</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="entropy-canvas" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div class="slider-row" style="margin-top:.75rem">
      <label>search progress t/T</label>
      <input type="range" id="entropy-slider" min="0" max="100" value="0" style="flex:1;accent-color:var(--purple)">
      <span class="slider-val" id="entropy-val">0%</span>
    </div>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:1.5rem;margin-top:.5rem">
      <span style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--purple-l)">● MLEvolve: <span id="eff-mlevolve">4.80</span> effective branches</span>
      <span style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:#6b7280">● Vanilla MCTS: <span id="eff-mcts">4.30</span> effective branches</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Vanilla MCTS (fixed exploration constant) stays near 4.3 effective branches throughout — even in the final hours, it still spreads effort uniformly. MLEvolve progressively narrows from 4.8 to 2.8, concentrating its late-stage budget on the two or three branches that actually matter. This is why it continues improving while MCTS plateaus.</p>
</section>

<!-- §04 Retrospective Memory -->
<section id="s-memory" data-code="memory">
<div class="section-no">04 — Retrospective Memory</div>
<h2>Experience Accumulation Without Extra LLM Calls</h2>
<p>Most MLE search frameworks are <em>memoryless</em>: each planning decision is made from scratch, with no access to what worked or failed in earlier iterations. MLEvolve's Retrospective Memory turns the search into experience-driven decision-making by retrieving relevant historical records before each planning step.</p>
<p>The memory has two components. A <strong>static domain knowledge base</strong> provides cold-start initialization: curated model recommendations organized by task type (image classification, NLP, tabular regression, audio), retrieved by keyword matching when a new task begins. A <strong>dynamic global memory</strong> accumulates structured records throughout the search — capturing the plan, generated code, execution metric, and analysis for every valid node execution.</p>

