<p>The hybrid retrieval combines lexical matching (BM25, good at exact keywords like error messages or model names) with semantic search (FAISS vector index, good at conceptual similarity). Reciprocal Rank Fusion merges both ranked lists into a single score without needing to tune a weighting coefficient manually. Critically, this requires no additional LLM calls for summarization — records are stored raw and retrieved at query time.</p>
</section>

<!-- §05 Adaptive Code Generation -->
<section id="s-codegen" data-code="codegen">
<div class="section-no">05 — Hierarchical Planning + Adaptive Code Generation</div>
<h2>Separating What to Change from How to Change It</h2>
<p>Existing MLE agents often rewrite the entire solution at every iteration. This is wasteful — a minor hypothesis ("try adding dropout to the classifier head") shouldn't require regenerating 400 lines of data loading and training boilerplate. MLEvolve decouples strategy from implementation with a planner-coder architecture.</p>
<p>The <strong>Planner</strong> operates at the module level using execution feedback, branch trajectory, and retrieved memory to decide <em>what to modify and why</em>. It outputs a structured plan specifying which modules to change. The <strong>Coder</strong> implements those changes in one of three modes, selected based on the current search state:</p>

<div class="step-list">
  <div class="step-box active" id="step-0">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Base Mode — Full Solution Generation</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Writes a complete solution from scratch. Used at the root node (first attempt) or after a Multi-Branch Aggregation creates a new branch. The agent starts with domain knowledge base priors and builds the entire pipeline: data loading → preprocessing → model → training → inference.</div>
      <div class="step-code">generate_full_solution(task, knowledge_base_priors)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" id="step-1">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Stepwise Mode — Module-by-Module Generation</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Generates code module by module following the planner's specification. Best for complex tasks that require multi-stage pipelines where decomposing reduces generation difficulty. Each module is generated and reviewed independently, reducing the probability of subtle cross-module bugs.</div>
      <div class="step-code">for module in plan.modules: generate_module(module, existing_code)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" id="step-2">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Diff Mode — Targeted Patch Editing</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Applies a targeted diff/patch to an existing working solution. When a runnable solution exists and the plan specifies a localized change ("increase learning rate, add cosine schedule"), Diff mode generates just the patch — leaving unchanged code intact. This produces stable, controlled modifications and dramatically reduces tokens per iteration.</div>
      <div class="step-code">diff_patch = generate_diff(plan.modifications, existing_code)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="step-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
  <span class="step-prog" id="step-prog">Step 1 of 3</span>
  <button id="step-next">Next →</button>
</div>

<p>A specialized Code Review Agent scans each generated solution before execution, catching naming errors, import issues, suspicious patterns, and metric inconsistency. A separate Data Leakage Agent checks for training/evaluation split contamination — a critical concern in Kaggle-style tasks where inflated scores would mislead search.</p>
</section>

